// The text of the English roadmap; docs/roadmap.md says the same in the repository.

import { pageHref, REPOSITORY } from "../i18n.rk"

/** A link to a section of the guide. */
func guide(id string) string {
    return `${pageHref("docs")}#${id}`
}

export comp RoadmapText() {
    return <>
        <h1>The road to 1.0</h1>
        <p>
            Rook is at 0.x: the language works, and this site is written in it, but the
            specification is a draft and may still change. The goal is <b>1.0, a language to use
            in production</b>, with a stable core and the tools a team expects. This page says where
            Rook stands, what is missing and in what order it will come.
        </p>
        <p>
            Rook does not try to be a wider TypeScript. Its place is real threads in the browser and
            in Node, checked by the compiler, and UI without a framework, in a language that
            compiles to readable JavaScript you can keep. Each phase below makes that place
            stronger.
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>New syntax comes from the list "The language for 1.0" below,</b> each piece as the
            maintainer decides it; other ideas wait for 1.0.
        </p>

        <h2>Rook today</h2>
        <h3>What is already there</h3>
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Concern</th><th>How it is</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Goroutines are an emulation on the event loop</td>
                    <td>
                        <code>go f(x)</code> runs <code>f</code> on another thread, a Web Worker or a
                        Node worker thread (<a href={guide("goroutines")}>Goroutines</a>). Inside a
                        thread, channels and <code>select</code> work on its event loop, as async
                        code does.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Data races, as in Go</td>
                    <td>
                        What goes to another thread is copied or moved with <code>transfer(x)</code>,
                        and the compiler checks that it can be. Memory is shared only through a
                        {" "}<code>SharedArrayBuffer</code> made for it.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>The runtime makes the bundle bigger</td>
                    <td>
                        A module imports only the functions of <code>rooklang/runtime</code> its code
                        uses; a program without channels, goroutines or markup imports none. All of
                        the runtime is about 19 KB gzipped, before minification.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>There are no libraries</td>
                    <td>
                        Rook imports any JS module and takes the types from TypeScript declarations
                        (<a href={guide("modules")}>Modules</a>): the libraries of Rook are those of
                        npm.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>A Rook component cannot go into a React or Vue app</td>
                    <td>
                        <code>@html-tag</code> makes a component a custom element that React, Vue,
                        Svelte and plain HTML use as any other
                        (<a href={guide("web-components")}>Web components</a>).
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>TypeScript code cannot use Rook modules</td>
                    <td><code>rook build</code> writes a <code>.d.ts</code> next to each <code>.js</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Debugging is blind</td>
                    <td>
                        Source maps: stack traces, in goroutines too, and debuggers show the lines of
                        the <code>.rk</code> file.
                    </td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Builds and tests</td>
                    <td>
                        The Vite plugin <code>rooklang/vite</code>; Vitest runs Rook code, goroutines
                        included (<a href={guide("tools")}>Tools</a>).
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>What is not there yet</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Stability.</b> The specification marks its experimental sections, but the
                compiler does not warn of deprecated forms yet.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The editor.</b> The VS Code extension shows errors as you type, completes and has
                quick fixes, but has no hover, go to definition, references, rename or outline.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The command line.</b> <code>rook build</code> and <code>rook run</code>; no
                formatter, no <code>check</code> for CI, no template of a project.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Debugging goroutines.</b> A deadlock is not reported, a goroutine left waiting is
                not shown, and the workers in DevTools have no names.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Numbers.</b> There are no benchmarks of channels and goroutines against plain JS,
                and none in CI.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>TypeScript under Vite.</b> The Vite plugin does not write <code>.d.ts</code>, so a
                {" "}<code>.ts</code> file that imports a <code>.rk</code> module there sees
                {" "}<code>any</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Comments in the output.</b> The generated JS keeps the names and the structure of
                the source, but not its comments.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Not locked in</h3>
        <p>
            Rook compiles to readable JavaScript with the same names and structure. A project can
            build its <code>.rk</code> files once with <code>rook build</code> and go on in JS. What
            stays is the <code>rooklang/runtime</code> import of the code that uses channels,
            goroutines or markup: plain JS under the MIT license, which can be kept as a dependency
            or copied into the project. The guide shows how:
            {" "}<a href={guide("adoption")}>Rook in an existing project</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>The road to 1.0</h2>
        <h3>Phase 0: say what is there (done)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>This roadmap, on the site and in the repository.</li>
            <li>
                A section of the guide on using Rook in an existing project: a <code>.rk</code>
                {" "}module in a Vite project, a Rook component in a React app, and leaving Rook with
                the generated JS.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Phase 1: stability (in progress)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                A <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md`}>changelog</a>. In 0.x a minor
                version may break code, a patch version never does. <i>Done.</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                Every section of the specification marked <b>stable</b> or <b>experimental</b>.
                Experimental features may change in any release; stable ones only through
                deprecation. <i>Done.</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                Deprecation: the old form keeps working for at least one minor version with a
                warning that shows the new form, then it is removed.
            </li>
            <li>
                Snapshots of the generated code of the examples, so that every change of the output
                shows in the diff. <i>Done.</i>
            </li>
            <li>Comments of the source in the generated code.</li>
            <li>
                The open questions of the design of components decided: the threshold of inlining
                stays a constant, and the names of <code>@keyframes</code> stay global.
                {" "}<i>Done.</i> The rest is the language for 1.0.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>The language for 1.0</h3>
        <p>
            1.0 is to be as complete as it can. Each item is decided by the maintainer first, then
            implemented.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Keys of lists in markup: <code>{"{for todo in todos with key todo.id { ... }}"}</code>.
                {" "}<i>Done.</i>
            </li>
            <li>
                A union of interfaces narrowed by a field of a literal type:
                {" "}<code>if d.kind == "courier"</code> gives <code>d.address</code>.
                {" "}<i>Done, with the narrowing of paths: <code>order.delivery.kind</code>.</i>
            </li>
            <li><code>derived</code> of several statements.</li>
            <li>Combining decorators, and <code>public ref</code> in decorators.</li>
            <li>A component that returns <code>null</code> for a wrapper.</li>
            <li>Refs in <code>{"{if}"}</code>, <code>{"{switch}"}</code> and <code>{"{for}"}</code>, and one ref in several returns.</li>
            <li><code>rook build --explain</code>: where the compiler did not inline, and why.</li>
            <li>
                The CSS of components in a file of its own, and the class of a style only on the
                elements that its selectors can match.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Phase 2: tools</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                The language server: hover with the type, go to definition, find references, rename,
                signature help, document symbols.
            </li>
            <li><code>rook fmt</code>: one style, no options, as <code>gofmt</code>; and formatting in the editor.</li>
            <li><code>rook check</code>: type checking only, for CI and pre-commit hooks.</li>
            <li><code>rook init</code>: a new project with Vite, Vitest and the settings of VS Code.</li>
            <li><code>.d.ts</code> of <code>.rk</code> modules under the Vite plugin.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Phase 3: debugging goroutines</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Deadlocks: when every goroutine waits on a channel and no timer can wake one, the
                program stops with an error that says where each one waits.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>rook run --trace</code>: when the program ends, the goroutines still alive and
                what each waits for.
            </li>
            <li>Workers named after the function of the goroutine, as DevTools shows them.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Phase 4: performance</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>
                Benchmarks of channels, <code>select</code>, goroutines, <code>go for</code> and
                updates of markup against the same programs written in JS by hand: the numbers on
                this site, and a run in CI that fails on a regression.
            </li>
            <li>
                Optimizations of the generated code only where a benchmark shows the need.
                Minifying is the work of the bundler.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>1.0</h2>
        <p>Rook is 1.0 when:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                the specification is no longer a draft: its core is stable, and deprecation works
                as described;
            </li>
            <li>everything of the language for 1.0 is in;</li>
            <li>
                the language server has navigation and rename; there are <code>rook fmt</code>,
                {" "}<code>rook check</code> and <code>rook init</code>;
            </li>
            <li>deadlocks are reported and goroutines left alive are shown;</li>
            <li>benchmarks run in CI, and their numbers are on this site;</li>
            <li>the guide shows how to bring Rook into a project and how to leave it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The same plan is in the repository: <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/docs/roadmap.md`}>docs/roadmap.md</a>.
        </p>
    </>
}
